{"name":"Biscuit Beige","hex":"#e6bfa6","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"biscuit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, flat, baked good which is either hard and crisp or else soft but firm; a cookie.","A small, usually soft and flaky bread, generally made with baking soda, which is similar in texture to a scone but which is usually not sweet."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fire (pottery) in a kiln, without a ceramic glaze.","To take part in the sport of tubing, riding down a river on an inner tube."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/biscuit"},{"term":"beige","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour, variously defined from a pale brown, to a yellow greyish off-white.","Debeige; a kind of woollen or mixed dress goods."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a slightly yellowish gray colour, as that of unbleached wool.","Comfortably dull and unadventurous, in a way that suggests middle-class suburbia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beige"}],"entries":[{"title":"Biscuit","blocks":[{"text":"A biscuit is a flour-based baked food item. Biscuits are typically hard, flat, and unleavened. They are usually sweet and may be made with sugar, chocolate, icing, jam, ginger, or cinnamon. Savoury biscuits are called crackers or biscuits for cheese."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b4/Bourbon_and_Custard_Cream.jpeg/330px-Bourbon_and_Custard_Cream.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Biscuit","wikidata":"Q13270"},{"title":"Beige","blocks":[{"text":"Beige is variously described as a pale sandy fawn color, a grayish tan, a light-grayish yellowish brown, or a pale to grayish yellow. It takes its name from French, where the word originally meant natural wool that has been neither bleached nor dyed, hence also the color of natural wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg/330px-Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beige","wikidata":"Q843607"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/BiscuitsAmerican%26British.png/500px-BiscuitsAmerican%26British.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/BiscuitsAmerican%26British.png/500px-BiscuitsAmerican%26British.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"North American biscuit (left) and British biscuits of the bourbon variety (right). The North American biscuit is soft and flaky like a scone, whereas the British biscuits are smaller, drier, sweeter, and crunchy like cookies.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABiscuitsAmerican%26British.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Kronborg%27s_ship_biscuit_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Kronborg%27s_ship_biscuit_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Kronborg%27s_ship_biscuit_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Kronborg%27s_ship_biscuit_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Kronborg%27s_ship_biscuit_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Kronborg%27s_ship_biscuit_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ship's biscuit from c. 1852 on display in Kronborg, Denmark","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKronborg's_ship_biscuit_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/PL_gingerbread_from_Torun.jpg/500px-PL_gingerbread_from_Torun.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign